Abstract The corrosion behavior of the inner surface of commercially available pineapple

11

cans （unlacquered tinplate cans）were investigated using accelerated pack test and

12

electrochemical measurements. In the accelerated pack test, the tin concentration in the

13

contents increased over time. The increase was greater as the tin-iron alloy layer became

14

exposed, and the internal pressure also increased. Electrochemical measurements

15

showed that the corrosion potentials of tin, iron, and the tin-iron alloy layer were

16

increasingly noble, in that order, supporting the corrosion behavior of tin in the

17

accelerated pack test. Based on these results, it is suggested that corrosion of the inner

18

surface of the can is represented by five stages.

