4. Discussion

19

As shown in Fig. 6, the concentration of tin in pineapple syrup increased over the course

20

of the accelerated pack test. This was clearly due to the dissolved tin from the tinplate on

21

the can inner surface side, as the characteristic X-ray intensity of the tin in the can inner

22

surface decreased over the course of the accelerated pack test, as shown in Fig. 7. In other

23

words, the amount of tin in the can inner surface decreased, and there was no tin in the

24

pineapple that had not been packed in a can. Generally, in atmospheric environments, tin

25

has a more noble standard electrode potential than iron and does not exhibit sacrificial

26

corrosion protection for iron. However, as shown in Fig. 10, in pineapple syrup, tin has a

27

less noble potential than iron, and studies have confirmed electrochemically that tin

28

corrodes preferentially over iron. As shown in Table 4, pineapple syrup was confirmed to

29

contain organic acids such as citric acid and malic acid. In an organic acid environment,

30

tin forms a complex ion [1] and is considered to act as an anode for iron.

