wiki/sources/ibrahim2025-metals-flavored-dairy-egypt.md:31:This study measured 15 trace and toxic elements (Al, Pb, Hg, Cd, As, Sb, Zn, Fe, Cu, B, Se, Ni, Co, Cr, Mn) in 180 samples of flavored dairy products from Egyptian retail markets using ICP-MS/MS, with health risk assessment for children aged 1-5 years. Products included UHT milk, pasteurized milk, milk powder, yogurt, drinking yogurt, and ice cream across chocolate, strawberry, peach, and vanilla flavors. Mercury and cadmium were below LOD in all samples. Pb exceeded the EU regulatory limit (0.02 mg/kg) in strawberry milk powder and pasteurized milk; estimated daily intakes of Pb and Al exceeded tolerable daily intakes for toddlers consuming strawberry drinking yogurt twice daily, with non-carcinogenic hazard quotients of 1.870-3.740 and carcinogenic risk for As and Pb of 8.40×10⁻⁴ to 1.68×10⁻³.
